## 5. PAGE-BY-PAGE SPECIFICATION

### A. HOME (/)
This page must make a strong cinematic impression within the first second and must not look like a standard template.

1. Hero (full viewport height)
   - Layered cinematic space scene: star field, soft blue nebula glow, the curved edge of a planet along the bottom (use a high-quality image slot or a refined CSS/SVG treatment), subtle parallax on scroll.
   - COSMO shown large and clearly, using the uploaded character reference image (placeholder frame until provided), with a soft cyan rim glow. Desktop: text left, COSMO right. Mobile: COSMO above or beneath the text, stacked cleanly.
   - Project name as the main title: BEYOND RELICS
   - Tagline: "Discover the Universe. Uncover Its Secrets."
   - Short description (2-3 sentences): "BEYOND RELICS is an educational scientific comic that follows COSMO, a fictional alien explorer, across the universe. Every discovery in the story is linked to real science, so you can read, explore and truly understand."
   - Primary button: START EXPLORING (goes to /explore)
   - Secondary button: READ THE COMIC (goes to /comic, or directly to the reader if the visitor has progress)
   - A small scroll cue at the bottom.

2. Project goals section: three or four concise goal cards (for example: Make space science accessible, Connect story to real science, Learn through interaction, Inspire curiosity). Short copy, icons, no statistics.

3. Platform highlights: a refined showcase of the four pillars with a short description and a link each: The Comic Universe, The Science Behind the Story, Explore the Universe, Knowledge Quizzes and Badges. Use an asymmetric, editorial layout instead of four identical boxes.

4. Featured comic preview: a cinematic preview of the first chapter (cover or first-page image slot) with a "Start Chapter 1" button.

5. Story-to-Science teaser: a visual explaining the flow "Story moment, then Real science, then Quiz", with a sample science link.

6. Explore teaser: a horizontally scrollable or compact row of the six topic cards with a link to /explore.

7. Mission teaser and closing call to action with both buttons repeated.

Scroll transitions between sections should feel smooth and cinematic but restrained.

### B. THE COMIC UNIVERSE (/comic and Reader)

Library page (/comic)
- Page header with title and short intro.
- "Continue reading" card (cover thumbnail, chapter, page, progress) if the visitor has progress.
- Chapter grid. Each chapter card shows cover image slot, chapter number, title, page count, reading progress bar, a "Completed" label when done, and links to its Quiz and its Science entries. Future chapters may appear as non-clickable "Coming soon" cards, clearly labeled and without buttons.
- Seed the site with two sample chapters using placeholder pages (Chapter 1 with 6 placeholder pages, Chapter 2 with 4) so the reader is fully functional and demonstrable before real art arrives. Each placeholder is clearly labeled "Placeholder — replace with original comic page".

Reader (/comic/:chapterSlug/:pageNumber)
Treat this as the most important feature. It must feel like a premium cinematic comic reader.

- Stage: full-viewport Deep Space Black, free of distractions. The page image is centered and displayed with contain behavior: preserve the original aspect ratio exactly, never crop, stretch or distort. Do not hardcode an aspect ratio; read each image's natural dimensions. Fit to height by default on desktop, fit to width on mobile portrait. Support tall and wide pages.
- Controls (top bar and bottom bar):
  - Top: back to library, chapter title, chapter selector dropdown, Mission Log icon, fullscreen button.
  - Bottom: Previous Page, Next Page, page indicator in the form "Page 3 / 12" (current page and total pages of the chapter), and a reading progress bar showing position in the current chapter. The progress bar is clickable and draggable to scrub, and shows chapter boundaries when displaying the whole story in the jump panel (which also shows overall position).
  - Jump to page: a compact input and a thumbnail strip/panel for choosing any page, organized by chapter.
  - Zoom: click/tap or double-tap toggles zoom (up to about 3x), pan while zoomed, pinch-zoom on touch.
- Immersive behavior: controls auto-hide after a few seconds of inactivity and reappear on mouse move or tap. Controls never overlap important parts of the page when visible (reserve space or keep them translucent at the edges).
- Navigation input: Previous/Next buttons, keyboard (Left/Right arrows, PageUp/PageDown, Space, Home, End, F for fullscreen, Esc to exit), swipe on touch devices, and tap zones on the left/right edges.
- Fullscreen: use the Fullscreen API. Where it is not supported (for example iOS Safari), fall back to an immersive mode that hides the site header and footer.
- Spread mode: include a Single page / Two-page spread toggle on wide landscape screens ONLY if it works flawlessly. Otherwise omit the toggle entirely. Single page mode is mandatory.
- Performance: preload the next and previous pages, lazy-load the rest, show a calm navy skeleton shimmer while loading, serve optimized responsive images (WebP/AVIF with fallback).
- Persistence: remember last-read page per chapter and overall; the URL always reflects the current page and is shareable.
- Story-to-Science link: when the current page has science links, show a "Science Behind This Page" chip in the toolbar and, optionally, subtle pulsing cyan markers positioned over the relevant area of the page. Activating it opens a side panel (desktop) or bottom sheet (mobile) with a short summary and a "Read the full explanation" link to the Science entry, without interrupting reading. Closing returns focus to the reader.
- Chapter end screen: a "Chapter complete" card with three actions: Take the Chapter Quiz, Explore the science from this chapter, and Continue to the next chapter (or "More chapters coming soon" on the last one).
- Accessibility: each page has descriptive alt text (from data), announce page changes with aria-live, full keyboard operation, visible focus states.

Content extensibility (critical)
- All comic content lives in a single structured data source (see Section 9). To add pages: drop image files into a chapter folder and add them to the chapter's page list. To add a chapter: add a chapter entry. No component or layout changes required. Include a README section explaining exactly how to do this in plain steps.

### C. THE SCIENCE BEHIND THE STORY (/science)

Purpose: connect events in the comic to real science and clearly separate fiction from fact.

Index (/science)
- Intro text. Entries grouped by chapter, with filter chips by topic (Exoplanets, Nebulae, Black Holes, Galaxies, Stars, Planetary Systems). Each entry card shows a title, a one-line summary, topic tag and the chapter/page it relates to.

Entry page (/science/:slug)
- Header with title and topic tag.
- "In the story" block, visually distinct (with a "STORY MOMENT" label, a dashed or softer border, and a thumbnail of the related comic page), describing what happens in the comic. Include a button "Return to the story" that goes back to the exact chapter and page.
- "In real life" block, visually distinct from the story block (with a "REAL SCIENCE" label, a solid cyan accent line), containing the simplified scientific explanation.
- Scientific illustrations: simple, clean SVG diagrams for general concepts only (for example a generic labeled schematic). Do not include numeric values or measurements in any diagram unless they come from a cited source.
- Space imagery: image slots with credit lines.
- Charts and graphs: include only when real, sourced data is provided. Otherwise omit them.
- "Did You Know?" callout box with one verified, sourced fact.
- Sources list: each source shows publisher, title and a link. Include relevant NASA reference links.
- Related science entries and a link to related Explore topics.

### D. EXPLORE THE UNIVERSE (/explore)

Interactive topic discovery. Six topics: Exoplanets, Nebulae, Black Holes, Galaxies, Stars, Planetary Systems.

- Card grid (responsive). Each card includes: scientific image (with credit), topic name, short description, one intriguing fact, and a "Learn More" button.
- Space Discovery Cards behavior: the fact is revealed by hover or focus on desktop and by a "Reveal fact" tap on mobile (keyboard accessible). Opening a topic marks it as "discovered". Show a discreet counter ("3 of 6 discovered") that feeds the badge system.
- Detail page (/explore/:slug): large cinematic hero image, overview, 3-5 key concepts, a short section on how scientists study this topic (general, with no specifics unless sourced), an image gallery with credits, a "Did You Know?" box, sources, links to related story moments or Science entries, and previous/next topic navigation.

### E. OUR MISSION (/mission)

Clear, persuasive and credible for the public and the jury. Structure it as a scroll narrative with these sections:
1. The educational challenge: complex science can feel distant and hard to approach (qualitative statements only, no invented statistics).
2. Why comics: visual storytelling lowers the barrier to entry and helps readers connect with abstract ideas.
3. How comics make science easier to understand: images show relationships, stories give context and memory hooks, characters provide emotional connection. Avoid claims such as "studies show" unless a source is supplied.
4. Who it is for: the target audience.
5. Learning goals: four or five clear goals.
6. Creative and technological value: the combination of original comic art, a custom reader, story-to-science linking, quizzes and badges.
7. How it works: a three-step visual flow (Read the story, Discover the science, Test your knowledge).
8. NASA Space Apps Challenge note, with a team-editable slot for the challenge name: "[CHALLENGE NAME — to be added by the team]".
Close with a call to action to read the comic.

### F. ABOUT US AND SOURCES (/about)
- Project summary.
- Team: a data-driven list of member cards (photo slot, name, role). Use obviously bracketed placeholders such as "[TEAM MEMBER NAME]" and "[ROLE]". Never invent names.
- Scientific sources: grouped list with working links to official root pages only: nasa.gov, science.nasa.gov, exoplanets.nasa.gov, images.nasa.gov, the NASA Exoplanet Archive (exoplanetarchive.ipac.caltech.edu), esa.int, webbtelescope.org, and spaceappschallenge.org. Use deeper links only when the team supplies them. Never guess or construct deep URLs.
- Image credits section listing the source for every external image used.
- Contact: show email and social links only when real values are provided in the data file; render nothing when empty.

