## 8. SCIENTIFIC INTEGRITY AND CONTENT RULES

- Do not invent facts, figures, dates, distances, discovery counts, quotes, studies or sources.
- Write science copy conservatively: general, well-established descriptions only, with no numbers unless a source is attached in the data.
- Every Science entry, Explore topic and Did You Know fact has a source field with publisher, title and URL. If no verified URL is supplied, link to the relevant official root page and note "Source to be confirmed by the team" in the data file (never shown as a visible warning on the live page).
- Never create deep links by guessing. Use only the official root domains listed in Section 5F or links supplied by the team.
- Keep story and science visually and textually separated at all times (STORY MOMENT versus REAL SCIENCE).
- Approved seed definitions to use for initial copy and quizzes:
  - Exoplanets: planets that orbit stars other than our Sun.
  - Nebulae: vast clouds of gas and dust in space.
  - Black holes: regions of space where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape.
  - Galaxies: enormous systems of stars, gas and dust held together by gravity.
  - Stars: giant balls of hot gas that produce energy through nuclear fusion.
  - Planetary systems: a star together with the planets, moons and other objects that orbit it.
- Do not write placeholder lorem ipsum. Where real project text is not available (team, chapter titles, challenge name), use clear bracketed placeholders.
- Add a short note in the README telling the team to review and verify all scientific content before submission.

